<p><strong>A gym stops losing revenue to expired memberships when every member has a tier, a validity date and a renewal that the front desk can see at the door.</strong></p>
<p><em>The scene below is a typical one, not a single named customer.</em></p>
<h2>The member who never renewed</h2>
<p>A member's plan ended two months ago, but he still comes three times a week and nobody stops him. Another renewed in cash and the receptionist cannot remember the date. The register is a list of names with a few ticks.</p>
<h2>Why it happens</h2>
<ul><li>Membership dates sit in a notebook or a spreadsheet.</li><li>Nobody checks validity at the door.</li><li>Renewals depend on someone remembering to call.</li></ul>
<h2>How it is solved</h2>
<p><a href="/features/memberships/">Membership software</a> tracks prepaid tiers, wallet balances, validity dates and renewals. Personal training can be sold as <a href="/features/packages/">session packages</a>, redeemed at checkout and tracked until used. Payments are recorded per branch, and the owner sees every branch in one dashboard. See <a href="/solutions/fitness/">software for gyms and fitness studios</a>.</p>
<h2>A quick fix you can start today</h2>
<ol><li>Record each member's start and end date in one place.</li><li>Check the date when the member arrives.</li><li>List members expiring in the next 14 days and contact them.</li></ol>
